FOUND s504c (probed first, it reproduces).  Repro /home/bernt/pcl/.claude/worktrees/agent-a41c97e5832f266e1/scratch/s504c/probes/q10.pl + q10d.pl: q10.pl `$cnt="gc"; do "./q10d.pl"; do "./q10d.pl"; print "main: $cnt"`, q10d.pl `my $cnt; sub inc10 { ++$cnt } inc10(); print "inc10=", inc10(), "\n"; 1;`.  perl: inc10=2 / inc10=2 / main: gc.  s504c: inc10=2 / inc10=4 / main: gc (base b7ec0093 was worse: inc10=ge / gg, main: gg).  The decl `my $cnt;` lowers to a p-defcell whose init is guarded by boundp (define-once, right for BEGIN-stored values) and no run-time reset.  Fix shape: the RUN position of a no-initialiser promoted decl stores a fresh undef box unless a BEGIN in the unit wrote it (the read-only/BEGIN case of #2633 must keep working: b2.pl).

=== s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 496df8b1 = MAIN-READY-113) === Rosetta census (#2104): 1432-Tarjan holds the SUB-level face of this cause -- a `my @x;` / `my $x;` with NO initialiser inside a sub, captured by a `my sub` helper, is promoted to a file-level cell, and the declaration emits NOTHING at run time, so the second call of the enclosing sub sees the first call's contents.  Moved to frequency ordinary (one real program is the evidence).
NEUTRAL REPRODUCER (run on the tree):
    sub counter {
      my $n;
      my sub bump { $n++ }
      bump() for 1 .. 2;
      return $n;
    }
    print counter(), " ", counter(), "\n";          # perl 2 2    PCL 2 4
    sub collect {
      my @seen;
      my sub add { push @seen, $_[0] }
      add($_) for @_;
      return scalar @seen;
    }
    print collect(1, 2), " ", collect(3), "\n";     # perl 2 1    PCL 2 3
  emitted: `(p-line 3)` and nothing for `my @seen;`; the body then uses `@seen__file__1` (the promoted cell) and the helper `pl-add__lexsub__1` captures it.  docs/not-supported.md ns-will-not-stay-shared says "Written with `my sub inner` instead ... PCL and perl AGREE (6 8)" -- true for a captured PARAMETER (re-assigned by the `@_` list assignment each call), not for a no-initialiser `my`: the fix is the one this task names (the run position of a no-initialiser promoted decl stores a fresh undef / empty value), at sub level as well as at file level.
THE PROGRAM: 1432-Tarjan calls a graph routine twice; its `my sub` recursion pushes components into the enclosing sub's `my @connected;` (and marks `%index`), so the second graph's answer begins with the first graph's components.  Rewriting the `my sub` as an anonymous closure in a `my $f = sub {...}` makes 1432 identical to perl -- its only cause.
LABEL: silent (ordinary: Rosetta 1432)
